<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Siris</h1><p class="st-meta">680 BCE – 530 BCE (approx.) · capital Siris</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Short-lived Ionian Greek city on the Gulf of Taranto, proverbial for wealth, destroyed by its Achaean neighbours in the 6th century BCE.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionia">Ionian Greeks</a> from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colophon">Colophon</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>, fleeing Lydian attacks, founded Siris around 680 BCE near the mouth of the Siris (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sinni">Sinni</a>) river, apparently taking over an existing native settlement. The poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archilochus">Archilochus</a> praised its land as fair and lovely.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Little is known of its government. Siris was famous for its fertile land and luxury, like its neighbour <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sybaris">Sybaris</a>, and became a rival to the Achaean cities of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/metapontum">Metapontum</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sybaris">Sybaris</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/croton">Croton</a>. Some scholars link the hill of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/policoro">Policoro</a> with its main settlement. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> records that <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/athens">Athens</a> later claimed a right to the site.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Around 530 BCE an alliance of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/metapontum">Metapontum</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sybaris">Sybaris</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/croton">Croton</a> captured Siris and destroyed it, reportedly killing suppliants at the altar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a>. In 433 BCE <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/taras">Taras</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/thurii">Thurii</a> founded <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/perinthus">Heraclea</a> on or near its territory.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="city state, 680 BCE – 530 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V42 H32 V34 H40 V42 H46 V34 H54 V42 H60 V34 H68 V42 H76 V82 Z"/><path d="M44 82 V66 Q50 58 56 66 V82"/><path d="M34 54 H40 M60 54 H66"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">city state</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Italy</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:46.17%;width:2.99%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>680 BCE – 530 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Siris</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ionic Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-605&amp;focus=siris" aria-label="Siris on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4896 756 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Siris lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5216 1012L5413 989"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5413 989h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5216 1012h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5216 1012h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Siris. <a href="/atlas?year=-605&amp;focus=siris">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

